<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Rebellion of 1857</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Indian Mutiny</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Rebellion of 1857 was a major uprising against the rule of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, beginning with a mutiny of Indian soldiers. The rebels proclaimed the elderly Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah II</a> as their leader. After its suppression the British exiled him, formally ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>, and transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to direct rule by the British Crown.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857.jpg" alt="Indian Rebellion of 1857" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author Cambridge University Press; Stanford's Geogl. Estabt. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Indian_Rebellion_of_1857.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> had been in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> since 1612, but its victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-plassey">Battle of Plassey</a> in 1757 began its firm foothold in eastern India. In 1764 it defeated the Mughal emperor Shah Alam II at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-buxar">Battle of Buxar</a> and gained the right to collect revenue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, and the Anglo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-maratha-wars">Anglo-Maratha Wars</a> brought more territory under its control.</p><p>The rising had no single cause. Resentment grew over invasive British-style social reforms, harsh land taxes, the summary treatment of some landowners and princes, and doubts about British claims of material improvement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-army">Bengal Army</a>, which recruited heavily among high-status <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and comparatively wealthy Muslims, was the largest of the Company's three armies. The immediate flashpoint is often linked to the Pattern 1853 Enfield rifle-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a>, whose greased paper cartridges were opened with the teeth. In early 1857 a rumour spread that the grease was cow tallow, offensive to Hindus, and pig lard, offensive to Muslims.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The rebellion began on 10 May 1857 with a mutiny of sepoys at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meerut">Meerut</a>, 40 miles northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>. Early on 11 May the 3rd Cavalry reached Delhi and called on the 81-year-old Mughal ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah II</a> to lead them, and he was proclaimed Emperor. Most accounts hold that he was coerced. His adhesion turned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> away from the rebellion, and the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a>, Travancore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>, did not join it.</p><p>Rebels took large parts of the North-Western Provinces and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>, Sir Henry Lawrence fortified the Residency, and after 90 days of siege the defenders were reduced to 300 loyal sepoys, 350 British soldiers and 550 non-combatants. Rani Lakshmi Bai of Jhansi, whose state had been annexed under the doctrine of lapse, became a centre of revolt. British forces retook Kanpur by mid-July and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> by the end of September 1857, and the rebels were defeated at Gwalior on 20 June 1858.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Both sides committed atrocities against civilians. Rebels killed British officers and civilians, including women and children, and British reprisals fell on rebels and their supporters, sometimes entire villages. Some estimates put the toll in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> alone at 150,000 Indians killed, 100,000 of them civilians. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahadur-shah-ii">Bahadur Shah II</a> was tried for treason and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, where he died in 1862, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal dynasty</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india-act-1858">Government of India Act 1858</a> dissolved the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> and placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> under the Crown. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal-army">Bengal Army</a> was scaled back and recruitment in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> increased. The name remains contested: Indian Mutiny, Revolt of 1857 and First War of Independence are all used.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian Rebellion of 1857” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Indian Rebellion of 1857</b> exposed the fragility of East India Company rule and led to its abolition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The British Raj was born from the crisis of the <b>Indian Rebellion of 1857</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The formal end followed the <b>Indian Rebellion of 1857</b>, after which the last emperor, Bahadur Shah II, was tried by the British and exiled to Rangoon in 1858.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 1880s Britain ruled India through the British Raj, established when the Crown took over the government of India from the East India Company after the <b>Indian Rebellion of 1857</b>.</blockquote><a href="/movement/indian-independence-movement">Indian independence movement · Background</a></li></ul></section>
